
=============================
SINGLETON DESIGN PATTERN (C++ FULL INTERVIEW NOTES)
FROM BASIC → ADVANCED (WITH CODE)
=============================

========================================================
1. BASIC IDEA (WHAT + WHY)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Only ONE object of a class should exist
- Provide a global access point to that object

WHY:
- Controlled access to shared resource
- Avoid multiple instances (memory + consistency issues)

USE CASES:
- Logger
- Configuration manager
- Cache
- Driver manager
- Thread pool manager

========================================================
2. BASIC SINGLETON (LAZY - NOT THREAD SAFE)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Object created only when needed

CODE:
--------------------------------------------------------
class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton* instance;

    Singleton() {}  // private constructor

    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

public:
    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        if (instance == nullptr) {
            instance = new Singleton();   // lazy init
        }
        return instance;
    }
};

Singleton* Singleton::instance = nullptr;
--------------------------------------------------------

PROBLEM:
- NOT thread-safe
- multiple threads → multiple objects possible

TIME:
O(1)

SPACE:
Heap object + static pointer

========================================================
3. THREAD SAFE (LOCK EVERY CALL - SIMPLE BUT SLOW)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Protect object creation using mutex

CODE:
--------------------------------------------------------
#include <mutex>

class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton* instance;
    static std::mutex mtx;

    Singleton() {}

    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

public:
    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (instance == nullptr) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
};

Singleton* Singleton::instance = nullptr;
std::mutex Singleton::mtx;
--------------------------------------------------------
Pros:
- Lazy initialization.
- Thread-safe using a mutex.
- Ensures only one instance is created.

Drawbacks
❌ Every call acquires the mutex, even after the object has already been created.
❌ Uses manual memory management (new without delete).
❌ Slower than the modern C++ solution.

TIME:
O(1) but slow due to locking

SPACE:
Heap + mutex

========================================================
4. DOUBLE CHECKED LOCKING (OPTIMIZED THREAD SAFE)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Avoid locking every time

CODE:
--------------------------------------------------------
#include <mutex>

class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton* instance;
    static std::mutex mtx;

    Singleton() {}

public:
    static Singleton* getInstance() {
        if (instance == nullptr) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

            if (instance == nullptr) {
                instance = new Singleton();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
};

Singleton* Singleton::instance = nullptr;
std::mutex Singleton::mtx;
--------------------------------------------------------

1. Thread-safe initialization
--------------------------------
- Mutex ensures only one thread creates the object.
- The second check prevents duplicate creation.

2. Better performance than locking every call
--------------------------------
- After initialization, most calls avoid acquiring the mutex.
- Only the first creation requires locking.

3. Lazy initialization
--------------------------------
- Object is created only when getInstance() is called.
- Saves memory if Singleton is never used.

========================================================
5. EAGER INITIALIZATION (ALWAYS CREATED)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Object created at program start

CODE:
--------------------------------------------------------
class Singleton {
private:
    static Singleton instance;

    Singleton() {}

    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

public:
    static Singleton& getInstance() {
        return instance;
    }
};

Singleton Singleton::instance;
--------------------------------------------------------

Eager Initialization Singleton

Pros:
✔ Thread-safe
✔ Simple implementation
✔ No mutex required
✔ No memory leak (automatic destruction)
✔ Fast access

Cons:
✘ Not lazy (created even if unused)
✘ May waste memory/resources
✘ Static initialization order issues

========================================================
6. MODERN BEST WAY (MOST IMPORTANT IN INTERVIEWS)
========================================================

IDEA:
- Static local variable inside function

CODE:
--------------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>

class Singleton {
private:
    Singleton() {
        std::cout << "Constructor called\n";
    }

    Singleton(const Singleton&) = delete;
    Singleton& operator=(const Singleton&) = delete;

public:
    static Singleton& getInstance() {
        static Singleton instance;  // lazy + thread-safe (C++11+)
        return instance;
    }

    void show() {
        std::cout << "Hello Singleton\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Singleton& s1 = Singleton::getInstance();
    Singleton& s2 = Singleton::getInstance();

    s1.show();

    if (&s1 == &s2) {
        std::cout << "Same instance\n";
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------------

Meyers' Singleton

Pros:
✔ Lazy initialization
✔ Thread-safe (C++11+)
✔ No mutex required
✔ No memory leak (automatic destruction)
✔ Simple and fast

Cons:
✘ Global state
✘ Harder to test
✘ Limited lifetime control

Why best:
- Provides lazy + thread-safe creation with minimal code.
- Avoids mutex complexity and manual memory management.

========================================================
7. COMPARISON TABLE (VERY IMPORTANT)
========================================================

LAZY (RAW POINTER):
- Fast ❌ not thread-safe

LOCK EVERY CALL:
- Safe ❌ slow

DOUBLE CHECKED LOCKING:
- Safe ⚠️ complex

EAGER:
- Safe ⚠️ wastes memory

STATIC LOCAL (BEST):
- Safe ✔️ fast ✔️ lazy ✔️ simple ✔️

========================================================
8. INTERVIEW ONE-LINER SUMMARY
========================================================

- Singleton ensures ONE object only
- Lazy = create when needed
- Best modern approach = static local instance
